  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Making Home(s) in Displacement is showing early but meaningful scholarly uptake, with 3 citations and 3,323 recorded downloads. Its strongest visible influence is in research on migration, refuge, placemaking, and housing, while its open-access availability appears to be supporting reach and reuse.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Making Home(s) in Displacement has attracted an early citation record and substantial download activity, suggesting that it is being read and taken up as a substantive contribution to debates on displacement, home, and spatial practice. The book\u2019s citation footprint is still modest, but the pattern is clear enough to show that other researchers are already drawing on it.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing literature points to a coherent area of influence: migration, refugees, integration, placemaking, and domestic or urban space. The book has been cited in articles published in venues including Gender Place &amp; Culture, Planning Perspectives, and Wellbeing Space and Society, indicating uptake across adjacent fields such as geography, planning, and gender studies. This aligns closely with the book\u2019s architectural and socio-spatial framing of home under displacement.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data provides a stronger signal than citations at this stage. More than 3,000 downloads were recorded, which is consistent with active consultation by readers beyond the small set of citing works. The book is available openly, and that access model likely supports this level of use, although the record does not let us separate who the readers are or how they are using the text.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also shows policy and sustainability relevance through alignment with Sustainable Cities and Communities, reinforcing the book\u2019s connection to urban questions about housing, belonging, and displacement. Funding support from KU Leuven and the Research Foundation Flanders (FWO) is documented, and the book\u2019s open dissemination through repository and publisher channels further increases its potential to circulate within both scholarly and teaching contexts.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the impact profile is best read as an early-stage but credible one: modest citation uptake, strong download activity, and clear thematic resonance with research on migration and space. The evidence suggests that the book is already functioning as a useful reference point in interdisciplinary scholarship on how home is made and remade under conditions of displacement.</p>",
